::MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.11
Question:
An insurance plan costs $80 per month. What is the annual premium cost?
{
=A. $960#Correct. Multiply 80 by 12 months to get 960.
~B. $80#This is one monthly payment, not annual premium cost.
~C. $92#This adds 80 and 12 instead of multiplying.
~D. $1,200#This does not match 80 per month for 12 months.
}
::MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.11
Question:
Plan A has a $500 deductible. Plan B has a $1,200 deductible. Which has lower deductible risk?
{
~A. Plan B#Plan B has the higher deductible.
=B. Plan A#Correct. Plan A requires less out-of-pocket cost before coverage begins.
~C. Both are equal#The deductible amounts are different.
~D. Cannot compare#The deductible amounts are enough to compare this risk.
}
::MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.11
Question:
What is a premium?
{
~A. money paid only after a claim#That describes possible out-of-pocket cost, not premium.
~B. the amount insurance always pays#Coverage depends on the plan.
=C. a regular payment to keep insurance coverage#Correct. A premium is paid regularly to maintain coverage.
~D. the same thing as risk#Risk is chance of loss, not the payment.
}
::MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.11
Question:
Why might the plan with the lowest monthly premium not always be best?
{
~A. it always has the best coverage#Lowest premium does not guarantee best coverage.
~B. it always has no deductible#Low premium plans may have higher deductibles.
~C. it removes all risk#Insurance reduces risk but does not remove all costs.
=D. it may have higher out-of-pocket costs when care is needed#Correct. A lower premium can come with a higher deductible or other costs.
}
::MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U04_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.FL.11
Question:
Annual premiums are $1,100 and the deductible is $700. What is a simple premium-plus-deductible comparison?
{
~A. $400#This subtracts deductible from premiums.
=B. $1,800#Correct. Add predictable premium cost and possible deductible: 1,100 + 700 = 1,800.
~C. $700#This is only the deductible.
~D. $1,100#This is only annual premium cost.
}
